  "rationale": "structural: The text prescribes the type's structure. That covers `#[non_exhaustive]`, the public `calls: u64` and `result_bytes: u64` fields, and the trait impls `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `Default` and `Serialize`. A type with different field types, a missing `non_exhaustive`, or a missing trait impl would break the declared shape, so these are real structural obligations. The only root, `crates/trust-but-verify/src/graph.rs#struct[ToolUsage]`, meets all of them. It declares `pub struct ToolUsage` with `pub calls: u64` and `pub result_bytes: u64`. It carries `#[non_exhaustive]` and `#[derive(Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, Serialize)]`, which supplies every listed trait impl. The root is marked `{impl}` for `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage`. The requirement has no other structural clauses. The phrase 'holds the cumulative usage for one tool' describes behavior that depends on how callers accumulate the values, which is outside this structural review.\n\nbehavioral: `requirements.sdk.graph.running-criteria.ToolUsage` states only a declaration (`#[non_exhaustive]` struct with `pub calls: u64` and `pub result_bytes: u64`) and a set of trait implementations (Clone, Copy, Debug, Default, Serialize). The cumulative counting behavior is delegated to the refining requirements `ToolUsage.calls`, `ToolUsage.result_bytes`, `ToolUsage.replay` and `ToolUsage.by_name`. Those have their own tests, so they need no test here.\n\nThe subject's test is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]`. It is linked through the `:test:` anchor on the requirement. It checks the declaration as follows:\n- `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md` lists `struct ToolUsage` and links to this requirement's declaration. `specified_exports` parses that inventory entry. `documented_exports` builds rustdoc for the crate and, for each public struct, calls `check_declaration` with the requirement's text.\n- `check_declaration` extracts the requirement's two code blocks.\n  - It asserts that the set of trait impls rustdoc reports (`trait_impls`) equals the set the requirement lists. That covers Clone, Copy, Debug, Default and Serialize, with no extras and none missing.\n  - It compares the rustdoc declaration (`signature_parts` over `RustdocSignatures::declaration`) with the requirement's declaration by set difference in both directions. That covers the struct name, the `pub` fields `calls: u64` and `result_bytes: u64`, and the `#[non_exhaustive]` attribute. A missing, extra or retyped field would fail.\n  - It separately asserts that a struct with public fields carries `#[non_exhaustive]`.\n  - It checks the member names with `member_names`.\n\nThe expected results come from the requirement text and the assertions enforce them. A change such as dropping Copy or Serialize, changing a field type or removing `non_exhaustive` would make the test fail. The requirement has no independent runtime obligations beyond those structural ones. I found no gap.",
